.Dd October 28, 2026
.Dt JAILCONF 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm jailconf
.Nd add or remove a value of a jail parameter in jail.conf
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl nv
.Op Fl f Ar file
.Ar jail
.Ar parameter Ns Cm += Ns Ar value
.Nm
.Op Fl nv
.Op Fl f Ar file
.Ar jail
.Ar parameter Ns Cm -= Ns Ar value
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
changes one value of one parameter of
.Ar jail
in its
.Xr jail.conf 5
file, in place: every other byte of the file, comments and layout
included, stays as it was.
It does for
.Xr jail.conf 5
what
.Xr sysrc 8
does for
.Xr rc.conf 5 .
.Pp
What the jail is configured with is read by
.Xr jail 8
itself,
.Ql jail -f Ar file Fl e Ar separator :
its parameters with global statements,
.Ql *
blocks, variables and includes resolved.
.Nm
parses the files only to find the jail's block and the bytes to change.
.Pp
.Cm +=
adds
.Ar value
to the parameter, unless
.Xr jail 8
reads it there already: to the end of the jail's own list when it has
one, otherwise as a new statement in the jail's block, written with
.Cm +=
when the jail inherits values of the parameter.
.Cm -=
removes
.Ar value
from the jail's own statements; the statement goes away with its last
value.
Values that the jail inherits are not touched.
.Pp
For
.Va ip4.addr
and
.Va ip6.addr
values compare as addresses, so
.Ql em0|10.0.0.1/24
matches
.Ql 10.0.0.1 ;
other parameters compare as text.
.Pp
The new text is parsed again before it is written, and written by
renaming a temporary file over the old one.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl f Ar file
The configuration file that defines
.Ar jail ,
or includes the file that does.
By default
.Pa /etc/jail.conf.d/ Ns Ar jail Ns Pa .conf
if it exists, otherwise
.Pa /etc/jail.conf ,
as
.Pa /etc/rc.d/jail
chooses.
.It Fl n
Do not write anything; print the change as a unified diff.
.It Fl v
Print the change as a unified diff after writing it.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Give the jail
.Ql web
another address:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# jailconf web ip4.addr+=10.0.0.12
.Ed
.Pp
See what removing one would change:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# jailconf -n web ip4.addr-=10.0.0.10
--- /etc/jail.conf
+++ /etc/jail.conf
@@ -1,3 +1,3 @@
 web {
-	ip4.addr = 10.0.0.10, 10.0.0.12;
+	ip4.addr = 10.0.0.12;
 }
.Ed
.Sh SECURITY
.Xr jail 8
reads the configuration and the files are parsed, includes and all;
then
.Nm
runs in capability mode
.Pq Xr capsicum 4 :
the edit, its check and the write of the one file, in its directory
opened beforehand, reach nothing else of the system.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr capsicum 4 ,
.Xr jail.conf 5 ,
.Xr arcana-agent 8 ,
.Xr jail 8 ,
.Xr sysrc 8
.Sh CAVEATS
A value written with a variable, such as
.Ql $ip ,
is removed only by that literal text.
